# Facebook through HasData

One tool, `hasdata_facebook_profile_getFacebookProfile`. It takes `handle`, which is the page name from its URL or the numeric id from a `profile.php?id=` URL, and returns the page with its post feed.

The payload carries two numeric ids. `profile.id` is the one a handle accepts. `pageId` is a different number and sending it gets you nothing.

## Counts come in two shapes

`likesCount`, `talkingAboutCount` and `checkInsCount` are numbers. `followersCount` and `followingCount` are the strings Facebook prints, and the label sits inside the string, so the values read `39M followers` and `24 following`. Strip the word and expand the suffix before comparing, and never sum a number and a string.

There is no rating field on a page.

## Language rewrites the strings

`language` changes the category and the other labels, so a category appears in German under `de`. It rewrites the string counts along with them, which is how `39M followers` becomes `39 Mio. Follower`. The numeric fields stay as they are. Set it when the answer is for a person in that market, not to get different data.

## Failure hides inside a success

A page that never existed, was deleted or is not public still answers 200. The payload then carries `error` instead of `profile`, and the call is billed either way. Test for the profile before reading fields, and report a missing page as missing.

## Paging drops most of the payload

The first response carries the profile, the photos and one post. A call made with `nextPageToken` returns `posts` and `pagination` only, so the profile is not there to re-read on page two. Keep what you need from the first response before paging.

Contact details are two different fields. `website` is the display text and `websiteUrl` is the address that resolves, alongside `phone` and an `owner` object.
